\paragraph{Model scale and architecture.}
Our primary experiments span 7--14B parameters: DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, and Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct (\S\ref{sec:scale-14b}). Whether the phase-modulated severity patterns persist, amplify, or attenuate at substantially larger scales (e.g., 70B) remains untested. Cross-architecture validation on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B (LlamaForCausalLM, \S\ref{sec:cross-arch}, Appendix~\ref{app:cross-arch}) confirms that both the phase gradient and strategy specificity extend beyond the Qwen2 architecture ($n{=}250$, all 8 conditions; strategy${\times}$phase gradient ratio $15$--$33{\times}$) but with substantially reduced absolute interaction magnitude (${\sim}26{\times}$ smaller than Qwen2-architecture models). The pattern has been tested on two architecture families (Qwen2ForCausalLM, LlamaForCausalLM) at two scale points (7B, 14B); comprehensive validation across more diverse architectures (e.g., Mistral, multi-latent attention) and larger scales is needed for full generalization claims.

\paragraph{Task coverage.}
We evaluate on three benchmarks spanning mathematical and scientific reasoning: MATH-500 (primary), GSM8K (\S\ref{sec:cross-benchmark}), and GPQA Diamond (\S\ref{sec:cross-benchmark-gpqa}). Cross-benchmark validation on GSM8K supports the phase gradient direction under importance-based eviction but reveals that random eviction is non-significant (CIs spanning zero), precluding meaningful ratio computation. On MATH-500, strategy specificity is quantifiable ($15$--$17{\times}$); on GSM8K, it manifests as a qualitative gap (significant vs.\ non-significant). This suggests that the phase-strategy interaction is robust in direction but task-dependent in form---complex reasoning (MATH-500) produces quantitative strategy contrast while simpler arithmetic (GSM8K) produces qualitative separation. Qwen GSM8K outputs were truncated at \texttt{max\_new\_tokens}${=}$2047, so Qwen GSM8K absolute values carry this limitation; DeepSeek GSM8K data is untruncated. Our task-complexity explanation for specificity attenuation (lower cognitive load $\to$ more uniform attention $\to$ reduced strategy sensitivity) is supported by within-benchmark correlational evidence but requires validation across a broader range of task complexities; whether different reasoning tasks---code generation, multi-hop QA---produce yet different attenuation profiles remains open. Random eviction on GSM8K exhibits numerical instability (extreme outlier II values), likely a systematic interaction between reduced-precision arithmetic and score-independent eviction.

\paragraph{Eviction strategy scope and H$_2$O confound.}
Our importance-based eviction uses cumulative attention scores (H$_2$O-style scoring~\cite{h2o-neurips2023}, Eq.~\ref{eq:kv-importance}), which creates an inherent confound: head pruning alters the attention distribution from which importance scores are computed, and eviction then operates on these degraded scores. The compound interaction patterns we report---including importance discrimination collapse and the phase-strategy crossover---may be partly attributable to this circular dependency rather than to compound sparsity \emph{per se}. Other attention-mass-based methods (SnapKV~\cite{snapkv-neurips2024}, PyramidKV~\cite{pyramidkv-colm2025}) share the same scoring basis and would likely exhibit a similar confound, whereas strategies that do not derive scores from the pruned attention distribution (e.g., learned eviction policies, recency-based heuristics) may exhibit qualitatively different compound interaction dynamics. Disentangling the contribution of score degradation from the intrinsic compound interaction requires testing eviction methods whose scoring is independent of head pruning; this is an important next step.

\paragraph{Symmetric sparsity assumption.}
Our analysis applies symmetric sparsity levels: the same fraction $s$ is used for both head pruning and KV cache eviction. A preliminary spot check under asymmetric allocation (Appendix~\ref{app:asymmetric-robustness}) supports that the phase gradient is robust to head/KV budget ratios, but systematic characterization of the full asymmetric interaction surface remains future work.

\paragraph{Phase segmentation validation.}
The \texttt{<think>} token validation of sparsity-based phase segmentation (\S\ref{sec:background}, Appendix~\ref{app:think-validation}) is limited to a single model (DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B) on a single benchmark (GSM8K, $n{=}48$). Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct does not generate explicit reasoning markers, precluding the same validation. Whether the high agreement ($\kappa{=}0.88$) generalizes to other models with explicit CoT delimiters, or to non-mathematical reasoning tasks, remains open.

\paragraph{Mechanism.}
Importance discrimination collapse is a correlational diagnostic observation, not a causal explanation: both strategies exhibit near-identical Gini drops yet severity diverges by $15$--$17{\times}$, indicating that Gini collapse alone is insufficient. Causal validation through controlled entropy manipulation is needed. The entropy redistribution component replicates on only 2/4 Qwen conditions, suggesting model-specific factors.

\paragraph{Perplexity vs.\ accuracy.}
Our primary severity metric is perplexity-based (II), chosen because its per-token granularity enables the phase-level decomposition central to our analysis; accuracy is binary per-problem and cannot be meaningfully decomposed by reasoning phase. Accuracy validation on MATH-500 (\S\ref{sec:accuracy-validation}, Appendix~\ref{app:accuracy-details}) shows gradual decline through $s{=}0.25$ ($\delta{=}-4$ to $-13$\,pp) followed by a sharp cliff at $s{=}0.30$ ($\delta{=}-27$\,pp) for Qwen ($n{=}100$, truncation $\leq 4\%$). DeepSeek accuracy deltas are confounded by rising truncation ($16\%$ to $76\%$); non-truncated analysis reveals a dual-regime structure but is limited by small surviving samples at high sparsity. Comprehensive accuracy evaluation across benchmarks remains an important next step.

\paragraph{Sparsity range.}
Our experiments span $s \in \{0.15, 0.20, 0.25, 0.30\}$, sufficient to establish monotonicity at the tested levels (JT test $p \ll 10^{-10}$) but insufficient to characterize the functional form of the auxiliary ratio $G(s)$ or identify potential saturation thresholds. More extreme sparsity ($s > 0.50$) may exhibit qualitatively different interaction dynamics. We do not report inference throughput or latency measurements, which are necessary for a complete deployment analysis but orthogonal to our severity characterization. Extended limitations in Appendix~\ref{app:limitations}.

\section{Importance Discrimination Collapse Details}
\label{app:discrimination-collapse-details}

This cross-model consistency (Table~\ref{tab:discrimination-metrics-full}) supports importance discrimination collapse as a robust consequence of compound sparsity. When importance scores lose discriminative power, thresholding the bottom-$s$ fraction becomes noise-sensitive: small perturbations change which positions cross the eviction boundary. In the limit of perfectly uniform scores, importance-based eviction degenerates into an arbitrary selection functionally equivalent to biased random eviction. The same pattern holds under random eviction (DeepSeek: $-0.031$ to $-0.071$; Qwen: $-0.019$ to $-0.046$), indicating that importance discrimination collapse is a consequence of head pruning \emph{per se}, not of the eviction strategy.


\section{Extended Discussion}
\label{app:extended-discussion}

\paragraph{On the predictability of phase-modulated severity.}
The compound interaction is not uniformly fragile---it is selectively fragile in a phase-dependent manner ($\widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\text{high}} < 0.06$ at $s \leq 0.30$).

\paragraph{Random masking as falsification control.}
At $n{=}500$, random eviction develops a detectable but weak phase gradient above $s \approx 0.20$: non-significant at $s \leq 0.20$ for both models, but significant at $s \geq 0.25$ (DS: $\Delta{=}0.26$--$0.51$, $p_{\text{Bonf}} < 0.006$; QW: $\Delta{=}0.39$--$0.91$, $p_{\text{Bonf}} < 10^{-10}$). Where significant, the random phase gradient is $2.7$--$17{\times}$ weaker than importance-based (restricted to conditions where both strategies are significant, $s \geq 0.25$), supporting that the dominant amplification is strategy-specific.

\paragraph{Entropy mechanism boundary conditions.}
Directly measuring per-head entropy shifts across architecturally distinct models---and identifying what training-time properties determine whether entropy concentrates or disperses under head pruning---would clarify the boundary conditions of the full mechanism.


\section{Pilot Mitigation Experiment}
\label{app:pilot-mitigation}

We explored oracle phase-aware budget reallocation, shifting sparsity budget from high-severity low-sparsity phases to low-severity high-sparsity phases ($n{=}30$ problems at $s{=}0.25$). Oracle recovery---the fraction of compound interaction eliminated by phase-aware allocation---reached 18\% (95\% CI: $[-39\%, 45.8\%]$; Wilcoxon $p = 0.002$), below the pre-registered 30\% gate for claiming practical mitigation. A heuristic variant achieved 32\% recovery (95\% CI: $[-22.8\%, 66\%]$; $p = 0.024$). \textbf{Critically, 95\% bootstrap CIs for both conditions span zero}, meaning neither recovery estimate is statistically distinguishable from no improvement. \textbf{Gate status: FAIL}---neither oracle nor heuristic recovery meets the pre-registered threshold, and neither is statistically distinguishable from zero improvement. These results indicate that phase-aware budget reallocation alone is insufficient to neutralize the severity gradient; effective mitigation likely requires joint optimization of budget allocation and eviction strategy selection across phases.


\section{Extended Limitations}
\label{app:limitations}

\paragraph{Model scale.}
All experiments are conducted at the 7B parameter scale.
Whether the phase-modulated severity patterns persist, amplify, or attenuate at larger scales (e.g., 70B) remains untested.
Larger models may exhibit different attention redundancy structures that alter the importance discrimination collapse dynamics (and the model-dependent entropy component may behave differently at scale).


\paragraph{Architectural scope.}
Our two models---DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B and Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct---share the Qwen2ForCausalLM architecture.
The cross-training-paradigm replication (\S\ref{sec:cross-model}) suggests that the findings are not artifacts of a single model's weights, though both models share the same Qwen2ForCausalLM architecture.
Pilot cross-architecture evidence from DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B (LlamaForCausalLM, \S\ref{sec:cross-arch}, Appendix~\ref{app:cross-arch}) suggests qualitatively consistent within-importance phase gradients ($d_z \sim 1.0$) but a substantially weaker strategy $\times$ phase interaction than in Qwen2-based architectures; random pruning also exhibits a non-trivial phase gradient on LLaMA, reducing the strategy contrast. This preliminary evidence ($s{=}0.30$ Wilcoxon $p_{\text{Bonf}}{=}0.029$; $s \leq 0.25$ non-significant) is consistent with the qualitative pattern extending beyond Qwen2 but is insufficient for definitive cross-architecture claims; quantitative impact is architecture-dependent. Comprehensive cross-architecture validation across more diverse designs (e.g., Mistral, multi-latent attention architectures) and scales remains an important next step.


\paragraph{Task coverage.}
We evaluate exclusively on MATH-500, a mathematical reasoning benchmark.
Different reasoning tasks---code generation, commonsense reasoning, multi-hop question answering---may induce different phase structures with different sparsity distributions, potentially altering the severity gradient profile.
Generalizing the phase-strategy interaction to broader reasoning domains is an important next step.


\paragraph{Statistical scope.}
Bootstrap confidence intervals use $B{=}10{,}000$ resamples (seed$=$42). The primary analysis uses $n{=}496$ paired problems for DeepSeek (4 excluded) and $n{=}475$ for Qwen (25 excluded); the permutation test (Table~\ref{tab:permutation}) uses $n{=}49$ from an initial profiling subset.
The severity difference distribution is right-skewed (mean/median ratios up to $43{:}1$), motivating our use of medians and nonparametric tests rather than parametric alternatives.
All four profiling-subset permutation tests reject $H_0$ after Bonferroni correction ($p_{\text{Bonf}} < 0.005$; Table~\ref{tab:permutation}), with CI lower bounds increasing from $0.19$ to $6.70$---supporting the effect direction at every sparsity level. At $n{=}496$, Wilcoxon signed-rank tests support all conditions with $p_{\text{Bonf}} < 10^{-13}$ (Table~\ref{tab:cohens-d}). The permutation CIs at higher sparsity are wider (e.g., $[6.70,\; 16.29]$ at $s{=}0.30$), reflecting genuine variance in per-problem severity amplification.

At $n{=}500$, random eviction on both models becomes significant at $s \geq 0.25$ (Qwen: $\Delta{=}0.39$, $p_{\text{Bonf}} < 10^{-10}$ at $s{=}0.25$; $\Delta{=}0.91$, $p_{\text{Bonf}} < 10^{-13}$ at $s{=}0.30$; bootstrap CIs exclude zero).
These effects are genuine but $2.7$--$20{\times}$ weaker than importance-based eviction at the same sparsity levels, consistent with a weak phase gradient that is detectable at sufficient sample size but not the primary driver of compound severity.

\paragraph{Sparsity range.}
Our primary analysis spans $s \in \{0.15, 0.20, 0.25, 0.30\}$; pilot data at $s{=}0.10$ and $s{=}0.35$ exist (6 total sparsity levels) but are not included in the main analysis due to limited sample size at these boundary conditions. Monotonicity is established at the tested sparsity levels (JT test $p \ll 10^{-10}$); the functional form of $G(s)$ between and beyond these levels is not characterized.
More extreme sparsity ($s > 0.50$) may exhibit qualitatively different interaction dynamics---the sub-exponential growth pattern suggests the severity gradient may plateau, but this remains unverified.


\paragraph{No throughput evaluation.}
We do not report inference throughput or latency, which are necessary for deployment analysis but orthogonal to our severity characterization.



